WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.839
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book one.

2
00:00:05.139 --> 00:00:08.653
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.953 --> 00:00:15.425
Unit twenty-eight: Succession Planning. Today you will describe required preparation with passive infinitives.

4
00:00:15.725 --> 00:00:18.343
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.777
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.617 --> 00:00:24.618
Succession.

7
00:00:25.617 --> 00:00:29.567
Succession means the process of one person taking over a role from another.

8
00:00:30.183 --> 00:00:33.112
Succession at the family firm was carefully planned.

9
00:00:33.717 --> 00:00:36.125
Your turn. Say: Succession.

10
00:00:38.967 --> 00:00:40.014
Succession.

11
00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:41.832
Successor.

12
00:00:42.817 --> 00:00:45.641
Successor means a person who takes over a role.

13
00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:49.236
Her successor had worked beside her for five years.

14
00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.746
Your turn. Say: Successor.

15
00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:56.717
Successor.

16
00:00:57.483 --> 00:00:58.589
Continuity.

17
00:00:59.583 --> 00:01:02.825
Continuity means uninterrupted operation over time.

18
00:01:03.417 --> 00:01:06.368
Continuity of service is vital in hospitals.

19
00:01:06.983 --> 00:01:09.821
Your turn. Say: Continuity.

20
00:01:12.667 --> 00:01:13.761
Continuity.

21
00:01:14.517 --> 00:01:15.495
Development.

22
00:01:16.483 --> 00:01:19.609
Development means growth of skills and capabilities.

23
00:01:20.217 --> 00:01:23.482
Leadership development programmes prepare future managers.

24
00:01:24.083 --> 00:01:26.724
Your turn. Say: Development.

25
00:01:29.567 --> 00:01:30.730
Development.

26
00:01:31.483 --> 00:01:32.577
Transition.

27
00:01:33.583 --> 00:01:37.220
Transition means the process of changing from one state to another.

28
00:01:37.817 --> 00:01:39.851
The transition took six months.

29
00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:43.230
Your turn. Say: Transition.

30
00:01:46.067 --> 00:01:47.021
Transition.

31
00:01:47.783 --> 00:01:48.993
Talent pipeline.

32
00:01:49.983 --> 00:01:53.980
Talent pipeline means a steady supply of people ready for future roles.

33
00:01:54.583 --> 00:01:58.359
A strong talent pipeline reduces the risk of sudden departures.

34
00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.672
Your turn. Say: Talent pipeline.

35
00:02:04.533 --> 00:02:05.964
Talent pipeline.

36
00:02:06.717 --> 00:02:08.159
Knowledge transfer.

37
00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:13.146
Knowledge transfer means passing expertise from one person to another.

38
00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:16.632
Knowledge transfer began a year before retirement.

39
00:02:17.217 --> 00:02:20.682
Your turn. Say: Knowledge transfer.

40
00:02:23.533 --> 00:02:25.138
Knowledge transfer.

41
00:02:25.883 --> 00:02:26.768
Stability.

42
00:02:27.783 --> 00:02:30.932
Stability means the state of being steady and secure.

43
00:02:31.517 --> 00:02:34.933
Clear succession plans protect organisational stability.

44
00:02:35.517 --> 00:02:38.099
Your turn. Say: Stability.

45
00:02:40.933 --> 00:02:41.958
Stability.

46
00:02:42.767 --> 00:02:46.638
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:47.483 --> 00:02:54.397
Visal, the head of a large NGO suddenly fell ill, and nobody knew how to run the organisation. How could that happen?

48
00:02:55.483 --> 00:03:03.430
Because succession was never planned. Leaders need to be prepared for transition long before it actually arrives.

49
00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:06.795
Surely you can't predict illness?

50
00:03:07.883 --> 00:03:18.024
No, but potential successors can be identified and trained in advance. Critical knowledge ought to be documented, not kept in one person's head.

51
00:03:19.117 --> 00:03:20.385
What should the board do now?

52
00:03:21.483 --> 00:03:32.077
Appoint an interim leader, then ensure that at least two people are developed for every key role. Stability shouldn't be left to chance.

53
00:03:33.233 --> 00:03:34.873
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:35.717 --> 00:03:38.413
Grammar focus: Passive infinitives.

55
00:03:38.713 --> 00:03:39.900
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:40.583 --> 00:03:43.059
Leaders need to be prepared for transition.

57
00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:46.077
Critical knowledge ought to be documented.

58
00:03:46.983 --> 00:03:49.227
Stability shouldn't be left to chance.

59
00:03:50.167 --> 00:04:00.600
A passive infinitive is 'to be + past participle' (need to be prepared) or, after modals, 'be + p.p.' (should be trained). Use it when the action matters more than who performs it.

60
00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:05.721
Be careful! Don't say: Leaders need to prepare for transition.

61
00:04:07.667 --> 00:04:10.978
Say: Leaders need to be prepared for transition.

62
00:04:11.278 --> 00:04:14.404
Passive infinitive: to be + p.p.

63
00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.818
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:18.633 --> 00:04:20.238
Planning for the Inevitable.

65
00:04:20.538 --> 00:04:25.928
Every leader eventually leaves, whether through retirement, promotion, illness or unexpected departure.

66
00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:34.854
Yet surveys consistently show that many organisations, especially family firms and NGOs, lack formal succession plans.

67
00:04:35.867 --> 00:04:45.401
Effective succession planning requires potential successors to be identified early and developed through stretch assignments, mentoring and exposure to strategic decisions.

68
00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:54.901
Knowledge transfer is equally important, since tacit expertise accumulated over decades cannot be documented in a single handover meeting.

69
00:04:55.900 --> 00:05:04.064
Organisations that treat succession as an ongoing process rather than an emergency are far better placed to maintain continuity and stability.

70
00:05:05.167 --> 00:05:09.087
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:12.170
The documents need blank before Friday.

72
00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:16.402
A, to sign. B, to be signed. C, be signing.

73
00:05:19.967 --> 00:05:22.280
The answer is B: to be signed.

74
00:05:23.417 --> 00:05:25.904
A person who takes over a role is a blank.

75
00:05:26.204 --> 00:05:30.496
A, successor. B, transition. C, continuity.

76
00:05:34.033 --> 00:05:36.428
The answer is A: successor.

77
00:05:37.583 --> 00:05:40.593
Passing expertise from one person to another is blank.

78
00:05:40.893 --> 00:05:45.475
A, development. B, stability. C, knowledge transfer.

79
00:05:49.033 --> 00:05:51.462
The answer is C: knowledge transfer.

80
00:05:52.617 --> 00:05:54.477
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:54.777 --> 00:06:02.354
A, The report must be finished today.. B, The report must be finish today.. C, The report must to be finished today..

82
00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:09.711
The answer is A: The report must be finished today..

83
00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:13.430
According to the reading, successors should be…

84
00:06:13.730 --> 00:06:19.276
A, identified early and developed. B, chosen at the last minute. C, kept secret.

85
00:06:22.833 --> 00:06:26.365
The answer is A: identified early and developed.

86
00:06:27.567 --> 00:06:31.113
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:35.192
Why should organisations plan for leadership change?

88
00:06:51.983 --> 00:06:53.344
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:53.644 --> 00:07:32.354
Organisations should plan for leadership change because every leader will eventually leave, and sudden departures can threaten stability. If successors have not been identified and prepared, critical decisions may be delayed and staff may lose confidence. Planning also ensures that knowledge is transferred rather than lost. For example, a hospital director's relationships and expertise need to be shared well before retirement. Succession planning is ultimately a responsibility to the people the organisation serves.

90
00:07:32.654 --> 00:07:34.282
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:35.183 --> 00:07:39.914
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and passive infinitives.

92
00:07:40.214 --> 00:07:45.211
Today's value is planning ahead. Make a plan and take small steps every day.

93
00:07:45.511 --> 00:07:48.291
See you in Unit twenty-nine. Goodbye!

94
00:07:48.591 --> 00:07:51.848
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

